Further, in accordance with the transparency and proactive disclosure principles under Section 4 of the Right to Information Act, 2005, and subject to law, the Government of Tamil Nadu should establish a monitoring mechanism for publishing these details for public information on the official websites of the district administration or local bodies.

Public Participation is Paramount

What is important is not merely that government officials participated in Gram Sabha meetings, but what actual action the government administration has taken on the demands and resolutions put forward and passed by the village residents and elected representatives.

Therefore, a situation in which Gram Sabha resolutions are merely recorded in registers and remain pending indefinitely must be avoided. An appropriate administrative time limit should be prescribed for each resolution, and the concerned departments should take action accordingly.

Request for Directions from the Hon'ble High Court
A request has been made that, in order to ensure appropriate action on resolutions and public-interest demands duly passed in Gram Sabha meetings throughout Tamil Nadu, the Hon'ble High Court, in exercise of its judicial review and power to issue a writ of Mandamus under Article 226 of the Constitution of India, should direct that District Collectors maintain time-bound status reports on such resolutions and submit them to the Government of Tamil Nadu and, wherever necessary, place such reports before the Hon'ble High Court.

It is expected that this would ensure accountability, transparency and time-bound administrative action in respect of Gram Sabha resolutions and further strengthen the direct democratic participation of village residents.

Request for Monitoring Through the Chief Secretary and Departmental Secretaries

In this regard, a further request has been made that the Chief Secretary to the Government of Tamil Nadu, the Principal Secretaries/Secretaries of the concerned departments, including the Rural Development and Panchayat Raj Department, and all District Collectors should be entrusted with coordinated administrative monitoring responsibilities, and that the Hon'ble High Court should issue appropriate directions to that effect.

For this purpose, it is requested that the Hon'ble High Court issue a writ of Mandamus under Article 226 of the Constitution of India, 1950, directing the Government to establish the following procedure in respect of resolutions duly passed in Gram Sabha meetings throughout Tamil Nadu:

1. Every Gram Sabha resolution should be fully recorded in the appropriate register, and a copy should be forwarded within the prescribed time to the concerned Panchayat Union, departmental office and district administration.
2. The concerned Departmental Secretary or Head of Department should obtain an action-taken report from the officials under their control regarding the resolution and record the action taken or the reasons for failure to take action.
3. Every District Collector should maintain a District-Level Action Taken Register / Status Report covering all Gram Sabha resolutions in the district, recording the reasons for pending resolutions and the time limit for further action.
4. The Chief Secretary to the Government of Tamil Nadu should coordinate the reports prepared by the concerned Departmental Secretaries and District Collectors, conduct periodic State-level reviews and issue necessary administrative directions.
